# Heap for the sbt JVM. `testUnit` runs every module's specs UNFORKED in this
# one JVM (CI never hits this — it splits the modules across runners), and the
# default heap OOMs partway through: the launcher dies with a Java heap space
# error, the remaining modules never run, and sbt still exits 0. A "green"
# testUnit was silently covering only common + web.
#
# ⚠️ DO NOT LOWER THIS. `testUnit` was MEASURED on 2026-09-04 at a 4092MB peak
# against this 4096MB ceiling — four megabytes of headroom. It is not a round
# number picked for comfort; it is the number the test layer actually needs, and
# anything smaller resurrects the silent-partial-run failure described above.
# To run something under a SMALLER heap, pass `-J-Xmx...` on that invocation (it
# is appended after this file and the JVM honours the last `-Xmx`) — that is what
# `scripts/dev-server.sh` does to make the dev server's OOM canary trip early.
-Xmx4g
# Fail LOUDLY instead. Without this an OOM leaves a misleading success: the run
# reports the modules that finished and says nothing about the ones it skipped.
-XX:+ExitOnOutOfMemoryError
# ...and leave EVIDENCE. `ExitOnOutOfMemoryError` makes the death loud but says
# nothing about what filled the heap: it prints one line and no stack. A local
# `sbt web/run` OOM on 2026-09-04 cost a long investigation that ruled out four
# suspects by hand and still could not name the retainer, purely because the dead
# JVM left nothing behind. A dump costs nothing until the day it is needed, and
# then it is the whole answer. `%p` (the JVM's pid placeholder) keeps reruns from
# overwriting each other — a bare directory gives every dump one constant name, so
# only the first is ever written. `target/` is sbt-created and gitignored.
-XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError
-XX:HeapDumpPath=target/oom-%p.hprof
# Keep AWT headless. The share-card and poster specs render with AWT, and on macOS
# a JVM that touches AWT without this registers as a foreground app and steals
# focus — yanking the user onto another Space mid-run. The forked web Test JVM
# gets the same flag in build.sbt; the two TestJvmSpecs guard both.
-Djava.awt.headless=true
# Format numbers the way production does. Twirl's `f"…%.1f"` uses the JVM's
# default locale: prod's image (eclipse-temurin, LANG/LC_ALL=en_US.UTF-8) gives
# "6.4", while a dev Mac's `en_PL` gave "6,4". PageTest renders inside THIS JVM,
# so its snapshots flipped between the two. The pin matches prod's en_US.
# Guarded by the TestJvmSpecs, like headless above.
-Duser.language=en
-Duser.country=US
